\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Distributional differences are visible, but their value depends on the evaluator.} \textbf  {a}, Concept: within a treated population, subpopulations respond differently and the outcome-governing state is often a minority one. \textbf  {b}, Mean-signature retrieval collapses a population to one vector; distributional retrieval keeps the full population. \textbf  {c}, A target population and candidate library define the inverse retrieval task. \textbf  {d}, Two candidates can share a mean yet differ in distribution. \textbf  {e}, Evidence tiers: objective-aligned (Class A), task-proximal (Class B), oracle-independent (Class C). This study reports all three. Its Class-C readout is an imported, semi-real functional oracle built from dose-response profiles in cell lines the transcriptional data never saw; a Class-C claim measured on the same cells would require wet-lab validation.}}{4}{figure.caption.1}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:1}{{1}{4}{\textbf {Distributional differences are visible, but their value depends on the evaluator.} \textbf {a}, Concept: within a treated population, subpopulations respond differently and the outcome-governing state is often a minority one. \textbf {b}, Mean-signature retrieval collapses a population to one vector; distributional retrieval keeps the full population. \textbf {c}, A target population and candidate library define the inverse retrieval task. \textbf {d}, Two candidates can share a mean yet differ in distribution. \textbf {e}, Evidence tiers: objective-aligned (Class A), task-proximal (Class B), oracle-independent (Class C). This study reports all three. Its Class-C readout is an imported, semi-real functional oracle built from dose-response profiles in cell lines the transcriptional data never saw; a Class-C claim measured on the same cells would require wet-lab validation}{figure.caption.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Mean-signature retrieval is the zero-variance limit of distribution-aware retrieval.} \textbf  {a}, Generative model $X=\mu +\lambda \epsilon $: the mean signature is the $\lambda \to 0$ limit. \textbf  {b}, Scaling only residual variance leaves the mean fixed. \textbf  {c}, Energy distance converges to the mean-distance floor as residual spread vanishes. \textbf  {d}, Under the implemented cosine kernel, mean cosine and CMap cosine are numerically identical (0.389); WTCS is a rank-based sibling. \textbf  {e}, The $\beta $-interpolation spectrum from mean-aggregation to worst-case, with energy equal to single-cluster coverage. \textbf  {f}, Metric-family hierarchy: every limit collapses to the mean.}}{6}{figure.caption.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:2}{{2}{6}{\textbf {Mean-signature retrieval is the zero-variance limit of distribution-aware retrieval.} \textbf {a}, Generative model $X=\mu +\lambda \epsilon $: the mean signature is the $\lambda \to 0$ limit. \textbf {b}, Scaling only residual variance leaves the mean fixed. \textbf {c}, Energy distance converges to the mean-distance floor as residual spread vanishes. \textbf {d}, Under the implemented cosine kernel, mean cosine and CMap cosine are numerically identical (0.389); WTCS is a rank-based sibling. \textbf {e}, The $\beta $-interpolation spectrum from mean-aggregation to worst-case, with energy equal to single-cluster coverage. \textbf {f}, Metric-family hierarchy: every limit collapses to the mean}{figure.caption.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Under objective-aligned metrics, distributional retrieval looks decisively stronger.} \textbf  {a}, Hit@1 ladder: energy 0.837 down to mean/CMap cosine 0.389, unweighted macro-mean over the seven (task $\times $ setting) cells of three retrieval tasks. \textbf  {b}, Per-task Hit@1. The advantage holds on the controlled and cross-line SciPlex3 tasks but \emph  {not} on Frangieh, the only natural real dataset in the macro-mean, where mean/CMap cosine (0.600) beats energy (0.578). The macro-mean hides this counterexample; we state it here and in the text. \textbf  {c}, Per-query regret reduction over \emph  {all} 765 partial-observed queries: median $+0.118$, 72\% improved (the $+0.119$ quoted elsewhere is the 621-query gate-recommended subset, and the two are indistinguishable, which is panel \textbf  {d}'s point). \textbf  {d}, Recommended ($+0.119$) versus non-recommended ($+0.122$) subsets: the gate does not concentrate the gain. \textbf  {e}, Alpha-crossover: the energy advantage narrows as subpopulations merge. \textbf  {f}, All five DART metrics show positive Class-A regret reduction.}}{7}{figure.caption.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:3}{{3}{7}{\textbf {Under objective-aligned metrics, distributional retrieval looks decisively stronger.} \textbf {a}, Hit@1 ladder: energy 0.837 down to mean/CMap cosine 0.389, unweighted macro-mean over the seven (task $\times $ setting) cells of three retrieval tasks. \textbf {b}, Per-task Hit@1. The advantage holds on the controlled and cross-line SciPlex3 tasks but \emph {not} on Frangieh, the only natural real dataset in the macro-mean, where mean/CMap cosine (0.600) beats energy (0.578). The macro-mean hides this counterexample; we state it here and in the text. \textbf {c}, Per-query regret reduction over \emph {all} 765 partial-observed queries: median $+0.118$, 72\% improved (the $+0.119$ quoted elsewhere is the 621-query gate-recommended subset, and the two are indistinguishable, which is panel \textbf {d}'s point). \textbf {d}, Recommended ($+0.119$) versus non-recommended ($+0.122$) subsets: the gate does not concentrate the gain. \textbf {e}, Alpha-crossover: the energy advantage narrows as subpopulations merge. \textbf {f}, All five DART metrics show positive Class-A regret reduction}{figure.caption.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The apparent gains do not survive independent evaluation.} The metric-class ladder, read row by row. \textbf  {Row 1, Class A $\rightarrow $ Class B.} \textbf  {a}, One scorer, one query set, only the class of metric changes: a Class-A regret reduction of $+0.129$ becomes a Class-B MoA-nDCG gain of $-0.037$. \textbf  {b}, MoA-nDCG gain by cell line, uniformly at or below zero. \textbf  {c}, Minority-coverage gain by divergence quartile: significant, and negligible. \textbf  {d}, The gate does not concentrate the advantage ($+0.119$ on 621 recommended queries versus $+0.122$ on 133 non-recommended). \textbf  {Row 2, why the gate cannot rescue it.} \textbf  {e}, The gate's structure-reliability axis is \emph  {anti}-correlated with true response divergence ($\rho =-0.21$), moving opposite to the quantity it is meant to track. \textbf  {f}, Recommended and non-recommended queries have indistinguishable divergence (Mann--Whitney $p=0.09$). \textbf  {g}, The real-data coverage advantage, task by task, across all 239 tasks: real but negligible (mean $+0.0018$) and confined to the constructed cell-line mixtures ($+0.0043$, against $+0.0003$ in the natural data and $+0.00003$ under predicted candidates). We plot the difference rather than a count of ``dominant'' tasks: the conventional $0.01$ threshold sits at 0.77 s.d.\ of the nonzero-difference distribution, inside its noise band, and under seed resampling only 2 of the 10 threshold-crossing cross-line tasks survive (circled: A549$\rightarrow $MCF7 Abexinostat and Belinostat, both HDAC inhibitors, above threshold in 10 of 10 seeds). \textbf  {Row 3, Class C, an independent functional oracle.} 103\ leave-one-drug-out queries, SciPlex3 at 10\,$\mu $M. The oracle is \emph  {drug--drug functional similarity}, not absolute potency: the rank correlation of two compounds' GDSC2 dose-response AUC profiles across the 966\ cell lines left once the three SciPlex3 lines are excluded, so it is measured out of context (Methods). \textbf  {h}, Energy reaches $\rho =+0.276$ against $+0.083$ for the correctly specified mean incumbent: the first oracle-invisible criterion here on which the distributional score wins. It is not a \emph  {distributional} win. A query-dependent scalar matching only the \emph  {size} of a candidate's response reaches $+0.232$ (hatched), and partialling that channel out leaves energy with $+0.097$ (dotted). Open circles, per-cell-line medians. \textbf  {i}, Per query, energy against that scalar. The cloud straddles the diagonal: energy wins $62$ of 103$\,$queries, and the nominal $p=0.073$ is anticonservative because queries within a line share a candidate pool. Scored against \emph  {absolute} potency instead, every ranking inverts and the magnitude scalar wins outright; that comparison poses a question a similarity retriever was never asked and is reported only as a confounder audit (Extended Data Fig.~5).}}{10}{figure.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:4}{{4}{10}{\textbf {The apparent gains do not survive independent evaluation.} The metric-class ladder, read row by row. \textbf {Row 1, Class A $\rightarrow $ Class B.} \textbf {a}, One scorer, one query set, only the class of metric changes: a Class-A regret reduction of $+0.129$ becomes a Class-B MoA-nDCG gain of $-0.037$. \textbf {b}, MoA-nDCG gain by cell line, uniformly at or below zero. \textbf {c}, Minority-coverage gain by divergence quartile: significant, and negligible. \textbf {d}, The gate does not concentrate the advantage ($+0.119$ on 621 recommended queries versus $+0.122$ on 133 non-recommended). \textbf {Row 2, why the gate cannot rescue it.} \textbf {e}, The gate's structure-reliability axis is \emph {anti}-correlated with true response divergence ($\rho =-0.21$), moving opposite to the quantity it is meant to track. \textbf {f}, Recommended and non-recommended queries have indistinguishable divergence (Mann--Whitney $p=0.09$). \textbf {g}, The real-data coverage advantage, task by task, across all 239 tasks: real but negligible (mean $+0.0018$) and confined to the constructed cell-line mixtures ($+0.0043$, against $+0.0003$ in the natural data and $+0.00003$ under predicted candidates). We plot the difference rather than a count of ``dominant'' tasks: the conventional $0.01$ threshold sits at 0.77 s.d.\ of the nonzero-difference distribution, inside its noise band, and under seed resampling only 2 of the 10 threshold-crossing cross-line tasks survive (circled: A549$\rightarrow $MCF7 Abexinostat and Belinostat, both HDAC inhibitors, above threshold in 10 of 10 seeds). \textbf {Row 3, Class C, an independent functional oracle.} \NCQUERIES \ leave-one-drug-out queries, SciPlex3 at 10\,$\mu $M. The oracle is \emph {drug--drug functional similarity}, not absolute potency: the rank correlation of two compounds' GDSC2 dose-response AUC profiles across the \CTXLINES \ cell lines left once the three SciPlex3 lines are excluded, so it is measured out of context (Methods). \textbf {h}, Energy reaches $\rho =\ENERGYFUNC $ against $\MEANCTRLFUNC $ for the correctly specified mean incumbent: the first oracle-invisible criterion here on which the distributional score wins. It is not a \emph {distributional} win. A query-dependent scalar matching only the \emph {size} of a candidate's response reaches $\MAGMATCHFUNC $ (hatched), and partialling that channel out leaves energy with $\ENERGYPARTIAL $ (dotted). Open circles, per-cell-line medians. \textbf {i}, Per query, energy against that scalar. The cloud straddles the diagonal: energy wins $\ENERGYWINS $ of \NCQUERIES $\,$queries, and the nominal $p=\ENERGYWINSP $ is anticonservative because queries within a line share a candidate pool. Scored against \emph {absolute} potency instead, every ranking inverts and the magnitude scalar wins outright; that comparison poses a question a similarity retriever was never asked and is reported only as a confounder audit (Extended Data Fig.~5)}{figure.caption.4}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The criterion decides the answer, from the inside and from the outside.} \textbf  {Row 1, the criterion decides.} \textbf  {a}, HIR-Bench's analytic flip boundary $\alpha ^*=B/(A+B)$ separates the regime in which the mean is a sufficient statistic from the one in which subpopulation structure can change the decision. \textbf  {b}, Circularity measured rather than argued, on the 13,440-instance grid: two feature sets $\times $ two cross-validation units. Features a method can \emph  {observe} at query time reach AUC 0.788 against a 0.643 majority-class rate (the rule on the colour bar); features derived from the benchmark's own latent utility matrix, of which the label is also a function, are no better than chance (0.400). Arrows give the interaction: pseudo-replication inflates the circular feature set by $+0.240$ and the honest one by only $+0.048$. Effective $n$ is 28 parameter cells, not 13,440, and every fold is single-class, so the 0.400-versus-0.5 gap is not itself interpretable. HIR-Bench's phase boundary is designed into its generator and its transfer to real data fails (Supplementary Note 1); its remaining panels are in Extended Data Fig.~6. \textbf  {c}, The same effect between two \emph  {real, external} criteria. An oracle is built twice from the same 20 surface proteins in the same 218,331 cells, changing only its statistical form (mean protein delta cosine, or energy distance between protein-response cell clouds), and the same two RNA rankings are scored against both. \textbf  {The winner swaps}: the mean incumbent leads under the mean-shaped oracle ($+0.242$ against $+0.146$), the distributional score under the distribution-shaped one ($+0.529$ against $+0.334$), independently in each of the three immune conditions. No scorer sees protein data, so alignment operates here between external, blind criteria. Hatched, the mandatory control: a query-dependent scalar matching only response magnitude, which compares no distributions. It climbs from $+0.125$ to $+0.352$ as the oracle turns distributional, the known magnitude channel ($\rho =+0.791$) behaving as predicted, yet energy still leads it by $+0.177$ there against $+0.022$ under the mean-shaped oracle. \textbf  {Row 2, from the outside.} The two gates on ZhaoSims2021, slice culture and biopsy from ten glioblastoma patients (validated marker assignment; Methods, Supplementary Table 3). \textbf  {d}, Gate 2, asked the same question in both settings: one drug against another \emph  {within one compartment of one patient} (36 splits, 4 patients), against the like-for-like constructed split. Bars are marginal medians; the annotated gap is the median \emph  {paired} difference, the correct and smaller statistic. The constructed ceiling of 0.692 was largely an artefact of \emph  {pooling} drugs into classes (grey): one drug against one drug, the same cells give 0.879. In a real tumour the information is present (ceiling \textbf  {0.923}) but unsupervised clustering cannot reach it (\textbf  {0.777}; gap \textbf  {$+$0.117}, 95\% CI $[+0.115, +0.135]$ from a patient-level cluster bootstrap, against $+0.007$ $[-0.008, +0.065]$ constructed; $k$-means 0.638, Leiden 0.780). The gap does not depend on the maximisation: with every fixed probe-clusterer pair and no selection anywhere it is $+0.122$ in the tumour against $-0.002$ constructed. Dropping the patient that supplies 30 of the 36 splits leaves $+0.123$ $[+0.098, +0.138]$ on the other three. \textbf  {Gate 2 in real tissue is an algorithmic bottleneck, not an informational one}, contradicting both our original claim and the cell-type comparison (0.964) with which we first tried to correct it; both are withdrawn. 30 of the 36 splits come from one patient and 39.9\% of cells are dropped by the assignment margin, so both numbers are optimistic. \textbf  {e}, Gate 1: induced response cosine, median \textbf  {0.566} over 17 patient-drug pairs, against \textbf  {0.014--0.044} in our constructed mixtures. On a divergence scale ($1-\cos $: 0.99 against 0.43) the overstatement is roughly twofold; the ratio of the cosines is not a meaningful measure of it. \textbf  {f}, The premise: over 18 within-patient drug pairs, mean-signature similarity ranks malignant-compartment response similarity at Spearman $\rho = \mathbf  {+0.878}$. \emph  {No $p$-value is given}: 15 of the 18 pairs come from one patient and reuse the same six drugs, so a test treating them as independent would be anticonservative. Both gates open here and the distributional advantage still does not appear: the two gates are necessary, not sufficient.}}{14}{figure.caption.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:5}{{5}{14}{\textbf {The criterion decides the answer, from the inside and from the outside.} \textbf {Row 1, the criterion decides.} \textbf {a}, HIR-Bench's analytic flip boundary $\alpha ^*=B/(A+B)$ separates the regime in which the mean is a sufficient statistic from the one in which subpopulation structure can change the decision. \textbf {b}, Circularity measured rather than argued, on the 13,440-instance grid: two feature sets $\times $ two cross-validation units. Features a method can \emph {observe} at query time reach AUC 0.788 against a 0.643 majority-class rate (the rule on the colour bar); features derived from the benchmark's own latent utility matrix, of which the label is also a function, are no better than chance (0.400). Arrows give the interaction: pseudo-replication inflates the circular feature set by $+0.240$ and the honest one by only $+0.048$. Effective $n$ is 28 parameter cells, not 13,440, and every fold is single-class, so the 0.400-versus-0.5 gap is not itself interpretable. HIR-Bench's phase boundary is designed into its generator and its transfer to real data fails (Supplementary Note 1); its remaining panels are in Extended Data Fig.~6. \textbf {c}, The same effect between two \emph {real, external} criteria. An oracle is built twice from the same 20 surface proteins in the same 218,331 cells, changing only its statistical form (mean protein delta cosine, or energy distance between protein-response cell clouds), and the same two RNA rankings are scored against both. \textbf {The winner swaps}: the mean incumbent leads under the mean-shaped oracle ($+0.242$ against $+0.146$), the distributional score under the distribution-shaped one ($+0.529$ against $+0.334$), independently in each of the three immune conditions. No scorer sees protein data, so alignment operates here between external, blind criteria. Hatched, the mandatory control: a query-dependent scalar matching only response magnitude, which compares no distributions. It climbs from $+0.125$ to $+0.352$ as the oracle turns distributional, the known magnitude channel ($\rho =+0.791$) behaving as predicted, yet energy still leads it by $+0.177$ there against $+0.022$ under the mean-shaped oracle. \textbf {Row 2, from the outside.} The two gates on ZhaoSims2021, slice culture and biopsy from ten glioblastoma patients (validated marker assignment; Methods, Supplementary Table 3). \textbf {d}, Gate 2, asked the same question in both settings: one drug against another \emph {within one compartment of one patient} (36 splits, 4 patients), against the like-for-like constructed split. Bars are marginal medians; the annotated gap is the median \emph {paired} difference, the correct and smaller statistic. The constructed ceiling of 0.692 was largely an artefact of \emph {pooling} drugs into classes (grey): one drug against one drug, the same cells give 0.879. In a real tumour the information is present (ceiling \textbf {0.923}) but unsupervised clustering cannot reach it (\textbf {0.777}; gap \textbf {$+$0.117}, 95\% CI $[+0.115, +0.135]$ from a patient-level cluster bootstrap, against $+0.007$ $[-0.008, +0.065]$ constructed; $k$-means 0.638, Leiden 0.780). The gap does not depend on the maximisation: with every fixed probe-clusterer pair and no selection anywhere it is $+0.122$ in the tumour against $-0.002$ constructed. Dropping the patient that supplies 30 of the 36 splits leaves $+0.123$ $[+0.098, +0.138]$ on the other three. \textbf {Gate 2 in real tissue is an algorithmic bottleneck, not an informational one}, contradicting both our original claim and the cell-type comparison (0.964) with which we first tried to correct it; both are withdrawn. 30 of the 36 splits come from one patient and 39.9\% of cells are dropped by the assignment margin, so both numbers are optimistic. \textbf {e}, Gate 1: induced response cosine, median \textbf {0.566} over 17 patient-drug pairs, against \textbf {0.014--0.044} in our constructed mixtures. On a divergence scale ($1-\cos $: 0.99 against 0.43) the overstatement is roughly twofold; the ratio of the cosines is not a meaningful measure of it. \textbf {f}, The premise: over 18 within-patient drug pairs, mean-signature similarity ranks malignant-compartment response similarity at Spearman $\rho = \mathbf {+0.878}$. \emph {No $p$-value is given}: 15 of the 18 pairs come from one patient and reuse the same six drugs, so a test treating them as independent would be anticonservative. Both gates open here and the distributional advantage still does not appear: the two gates are necessary, not sufficient}{figure.caption.5}{}}
