\citation{CellFlow}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The information condition is a two-gate criterion: candidate populations must show differential response, and that structure must be identifiable.} \textbf  {a}, The predict-then-rank design: a held-out query is scored against candidate populations that are either observed or produced by a perturbation predictor, and the two gates are the two properties such a candidate population must have. \textbf  {b}, Predictor-specific retrieval gain (nDCG@10, DART minus mean). The gain is small and \emph  {not} consistent in sign: $-0.071$ (nearest-neighbour), $-0.016$ (average-effect), $+0.027$ (latent-linear). \textbf  {c}, Gate 1. Predicted candidate populations \emph  {retain} baseline subpopulation structure (variance ratio 0.142 versus 0.138 for real ones, left axis) but do not reproduce \emph  {divergence}: the induced response cosine between the two subpopulations is 0.19--0.37 for predicted populations against 0.014 for real ones (right axis). An additive predictor adds one delta vector to every cell of a context, so within that context its two subpopulations have identical responses: cosine exactly 1, divergence exactly zero. That algebraic ceiling is marked on the right axis. \textbf  {The predicted values shown here do not measure it, and we do not interpret them.} These candidate populations are blends of two cell lines, so their subpopulations sit in different contexts and an additive predictor emits a different delta for each; and for a latent model the response taken against real control cells also carries the generator's reconstruction bias. Measured within one context and against the model's own baseline, an additive predictor returns exactly 1.000000, as the algebra requires (Methods). \textbf  {d}, Structure diagnostics, predicted relative to real. \textbf  {e}, Gate 2, on a known bimodal mixture (K562, HDAC-class versus JAK-class cells). All methods are scored in one unit, best-permutation accuracy against the true labels, with the label matching given to each clusterer for free. Unsupervised recovery is poor ($k$-means 0.632 and Gaussian mixtures 0.661, both handed the true $k$; Leiden 0.674; HDBSCAN 0.500, finding no density structure at all), but the \emph  {supervised ceiling}, a classifier given the ground-truth partition with its representation fit inside the training fold, reaches only 0.692 (AUC 0.752), and a nonlinear learner does not beat a linear one (forest 0.669, $k$NN 0.605, logistic 0.685). The gap between the ceiling and the best unsupervised method is \textbf  {0.018}: clustering is already extracting essentially everything this representation holds, so \emph  {within this benchmark} the limit is informational rather than algorithmic. That qualifier is load-bearing, and two later corrections dismantle what it appears to license. The gap is an \emph  {underestimate}, because the clusterers are given the easier transductive representation and the probe the harder in-fold one, which shrinks it (Methods). And this 0.692 ceiling is largely an artefact of \emph  {pooling} several HDAC and several JAK compounds into two classes: the same cells split one drug against one drug give 0.879, and in a patient's tumour the same drug-response question gives a ceiling of 0.923 with unsupervised clustering reaching only 0.777 (Fig.~\ref {fig:5}d). In real tissue the limit is algorithmic, not informational, and the reading offered here is withdrawn. \textbf  {f}, The control that licenses reading panel \textbf  {e} at all. Raise the separation between the two states artificially and the probe pulls away from clustering exactly as it should (ceiling 0.828 versus 0.714 at $1.5\times $; 0.924 versus 0.762 at $2\times $), so its failure to pull away on this mixture (leftmost, separation $=1$) is a measurement rather than an insensitive probe. \textbf  {g}, The conditional advantage a working information condition would produce is absent. DART-minus-mean MoA-nDCG gain by \emph  {true} response-divergence quartile (bars are means; Benjamini--Hochberg corrected). No stratum is positive. The lowest-divergence quartile is significantly \emph  {negative} (mean $-0.082$, median $-0.030$, $q = 5.8\times 10^{-5}$, $n = 166$); the rest have a median of exactly zero and means of $-0.041$, $-0.020$ and $+0.003$, none significant. Where the theory predicts the largest gain there is none. Stratifying instead by the Gate-2 variable leaves the advantage flat (Supplementary Note 2).}}{20}{figure.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:6}{{6}{20}{\textbf {The information condition is a two-gate criterion: candidate populations must show differential response, and that structure must be identifiable.} \textbf {a}, The predict-then-rank design: a held-out query is scored against candidate populations that are either observed or produced by a perturbation predictor, and the two gates are the two properties such a candidate population must have. \textbf {b}, Predictor-specific retrieval gain (nDCG@10, DART minus mean). The gain is small and \emph {not} consistent in sign: $-0.071$ (nearest-neighbour), $-0.016$ (average-effect), $+0.027$ (latent-linear). \textbf {c}, Gate 1. Predicted candidate populations \emph {retain} baseline subpopulation structure (variance ratio 0.142 versus 0.138 for real ones, left axis) but do not reproduce \emph {divergence}: the induced response cosine between the two subpopulations is 0.19--0.37 for predicted populations against 0.014 for real ones (right axis). An additive predictor adds one delta vector to every cell of a context, so within that context its two subpopulations have identical responses: cosine exactly 1, divergence exactly zero. That algebraic ceiling is marked on the right axis. \textbf {The predicted values shown here do not measure it, and we do not interpret them.} These candidate populations are blends of two cell lines, so their subpopulations sit in different contexts and an additive predictor emits a different delta for each; and for a latent model the response taken against real control cells also carries the generator's reconstruction bias. Measured within one context and against the model's own baseline, an additive predictor returns exactly 1.000000, as the algebra requires (Methods). \textbf {d}, Structure diagnostics, predicted relative to real. \textbf {e}, Gate 2, on a known bimodal mixture (K562, HDAC-class versus JAK-class cells). All methods are scored in one unit, best-permutation accuracy against the true labels, with the label matching given to each clusterer for free. Unsupervised recovery is poor ($k$-means 0.632 and Gaussian mixtures 0.661, both handed the true $k$; Leiden 0.674; HDBSCAN 0.500, finding no density structure at all), but the \emph {supervised ceiling}, a classifier given the ground-truth partition with its representation fit inside the training fold, reaches only 0.692 (AUC 0.752), and a nonlinear learner does not beat a linear one (forest 0.669, $k$NN 0.605, logistic 0.685). The gap between the ceiling and the best unsupervised method is \textbf {0.018}: clustering is already extracting essentially everything this representation holds, so \emph {within this benchmark} the limit is informational rather than algorithmic. That qualifier is load-bearing, and two later corrections dismantle what it appears to license. The gap is an \emph {underestimate}, because the clusterers are given the easier transductive representation and the probe the harder in-fold one, which shrinks it (Methods). And this 0.692 ceiling is largely an artefact of \emph {pooling} several HDAC and several JAK compounds into two classes: the same cells split one drug against one drug give 0.879, and in a patient's tumour the same drug-response question gives a ceiling of 0.923 with unsupervised clustering reaching only 0.777 (Fig.~\ref {fig:5}d). In real tissue the limit is algorithmic, not informational, and the reading offered here is withdrawn. \textbf {f}, The control that licenses reading panel \textbf {e} at all. Raise the separation between the two states artificially and the probe pulls away from clustering exactly as it should (ceiling 0.828 versus 0.714 at $1.5\times $; 0.924 versus 0.762 at $2\times $), so its failure to pull away on this mixture (leftmost, separation $=1$) is a measurement rather than an insensitive probe. \textbf {g}, The conditional advantage a working information condition would produce is absent. DART-minus-mean MoA-nDCG gain by \emph {true} response-divergence quartile (bars are means; Benjamini--Hochberg corrected). No stratum is positive. The lowest-divergence quartile is significantly \emph {negative} (mean $-0.082$, median $-0.030$, $q = 5.8\times 10^{-5}$, $n = 166$); the rest have a median of exactly zero and means of $-0.041$, $-0.020$ and $+0.003$, none significant. Where the theory predicts the largest gain there is none. Stratifying instead by the Gate-2 variable leaves the advantage flat (Supplementary Note 2)}{figure.caption.6}{}}
